+    "value": "Top band (5-6): Thorough discussion. E.g., 3D printing does not require a mould, so setup costs are low, but each unit takes time to print and finish — suitable for small batches only. Injection moulding requires a mould with an upfront setup cost but produces each unit in seconds with excellent consistency and surface finish. At 500 units, the most cost-effective process depends on the part design, tooling and supplier costs. 3D printing may be preferable for very small quantities or complex geometries that cannot be moulded.\nMid band (3-4): Adequate discussion with some comparison.\nLower band (1-2): Basic identification of one method with limited justification.",
